      "side": 1,
      "message": "I wouldn\u0027t want to rely on another repo. Need to replace this with something else.\n\nSo, I went to https://tools.ietf.org/html/rfc7517#appendix-A.1 and picked X and Y from example A.1:\n\n    base64X \u003d \"MKBCTNIcKUSDii11ySs3526iDZ8AiTo7Tu6KPAqv7D4\"\n    base64Y \u003d \"4Etl6SRW2YiLUrN5vfvVHuhp7x8PxltmWWlbbM4IFyM\"\n\nThen, the expected value can be computed as follows:\n\n    echo -n \u0027{\"crv\":\"P-256\",\"kty\":\"EC\",\"x\":\"MKBCTNIcKUSDii11ySs3526iDZ8AiTo7Tu6KPAqv7D4\",\"y\":\"4Etl6SRW2YiLUrN5vfvVHuhp7x8PxltmWWlbbM4IFyM\"}\u0027 | \\\n    openssl dgst -binary -sha256 | \\\n    base64 | \\\n    tr -d \u0027\u003d\u0027 | tr \u0027/+\u0027 \u0027_-\u0027\n\nNote that the curve is P-256.\n\nBoth X and Y in this example are 32 bytes. It would be nice to have another (or use alternative to this) set of X/Y values to verify the padding when you\u0027re constructing the EC JWK.",
      "revId": "4cefa7a3af5e260d86398f1fe8654279cda6d7df",
      "serverId": "62eb7196-b449-3ce5-99f1-c037f21e1705",
      "unresolved": false
    },
